//  Vectors carried at once by one invocation. A constant, so that the loops
//  over it unroll and the accumulators stay in registers; making it the
//  batch length instead would put them in memory and give back what the
//  batching just bought.
//  This source is compiled three times, and two of those compilations
//  define a width. A generated token is one vector against every matrix in
//  the model, and with GROUP at eight it carries eight accumulators, reads
//  eight offsets and reserves eight kilobytes of shared memory for a
//  reduction seven eighths of which is thrown away. The engine binds the
//  narrow pipeline when the batch is one and the wide one otherwise, so
//  neither pays for the other -- the same trade matrix_product.comp makes
//  between its two compilations, and for the same measured reason.
//
//  WIDER carries sixteen, for the case the other two leave badly served: a
//  round of nine to thirty-one sequences, which is two dispatches at eight
//  and therefore two passes over every weight in the model, and which the
//  matrix product does not take either because a tile is a hundred and
//  twenty-eight vectors wide and would be mostly the zeros the rounding
//  invented. Sixteen accumulators and sixteen kilobytes of shared memory
//  buy one pass instead of two. That is a trade of occupancy against
//  bandwidth and the measurement is what settles it, not the arithmetic.
//  How many vectors an invocation carries, as a specialization constant
//  rather than four compiled copies of this file.
//
//  It was four modules -- one, four, eight and sixteen -- and each of them
//  is ninety-six kilobytes of SPIR-V in the generated Ada, which put that
//  file over the size this repository accepts. A specialization constant is
//  fixed when the pipeline is made, so the loops below still unroll and the
//  arrays below still have a constant size; what it costs is nothing and
//  what it saves is three copies of every format's decoder.
//
//  llama.cpp compiles one of these for every count from one to eight and
//  indexes them by it. One module that takes the count is the same idea
//  without the four hundred kilobytes.

//  Invocations that share one row.
//
//  One invocation a row is the shape this began with, and it reads memory
//  the worst way a device can be asked to: at any step the lanes of a wave
//  want one byte each from addresses a whole row apart, so sixty-four lanes
//  make sixty-four transactions to fetch what would fit in one. The part is
//  not short of bandwidth -- 7.8 gigabytes a second of about ninety -- it is
//  short of transactions, which is what that arrangement spends.
//
//  Eight lanes to a row, each taking every eighth block, is what this does.
//
//  Every eighth block rather than every eighth element, and the difference
//  is the whole of what a block costs besides its quants: a scale to decode
//  and an offset to work out, once per block. Divide inside a block and all
//  eight lanes do that work for the same block -- eight times the scale
//  decodes for an eighth of the multiplies each, which measured 0.603 s
//  against 0.520 for the shape it replaced. Divide by block and it stays
//  done once. Eight lanes then read from addresses a block apart rather
//  than a row apart, which for eight-bit blocks is two hundred and seventy
//  bytes rather than two thousand: a handful of cache lines instead of
//  eight separate transactions.
//
//  What it costs is a reduction, and where that reduction goes is the whole
//  difference between this and the attempt that failed. That one divided a
//  row's blocks across lanes and reduced them at every block, through a
//  barrier each time: 4.300 s at sixty-four lanes against 1.937 for the
//  shape it replaced. This reduces once, at the end of the row, through one
//  barrier for the whole workgroup -- sixty-four blocks of arithmetic per
//  barrier rather than one.
//
//  Shared memory rather than a subgroup add, which would be fewer
//  instructions and needs SPIR-V 1.3 and so a Vulkan 1.1 instance. This
//  program asks for 1.0 and a device that offers only 1.0 runs everything
//  else here; trading that away for one reduction a row is not a trade
//  worth making.
const uint LANES = 8u;

//  Each invocation's share of its row, for the reduction below. Sized by
//  the workgroup width declared above, which the two have to agree about.
//  Room for the widest the constant above may be set to. A workgroup
//  narrower than that leaves the tail of this unread, which is a kilobyte
//  at a batch of one and worth less than a second set of words.
shared float shares[256u * GROUP];
//  The sixteen levels a non-linear four-bit quant may take. A nibble in
//  IQ4_NL and IQ4_XS is not a number but an index into this, and the table
//  belongs to the format rather than to any file.
const int LEVELS[16] = int[16](-127, -104, -83, -65, -49, -35, -22, -10,
                                  1,   13,  25,  38,  53,  69,  89, 113);

//  MXFP4's own sixteen, which are the E2M1 floating-point values at twice
//  their size: 0, 0.5, 1, 1.5, 2, 3, 4, 6 and the same again negated.
//  Doubling them keeps the table whole, and the scale carries the halving
//  -- which is why an exponent byte is read as two to the byte less a
//  hundred and twenty-eight rather than a hundred and twenty-seven. The
//  same table and the same reading as the processor's decoder.
const int FOURS[16] = int[16](0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12,
                              0, -1, -2, -3, -4, -6, -8, -12);

//  Where each of this invocation's vectors begins, and what it has
//  accumulated. Both are at file scope so that the accumulation below can be
//  a function rather than fifteen copies of the same three lines: a GLSL
//  parameter of array type is copied at every call, and copying eight
//  offsets per element would cost more than the decoding does.
uint from[GROUP];
float sum[GROUP];

//  One weight against every vector in the batch.
void accumulate(uint col, float weight) {
    for (uint k = 0u; k < GROUP; ++k) {
        sum[k] += weight * x[from[k] + col];
    }
}

//  One byte of the weights. Blocks are 18 to 210 bytes long, so a block
//  boundary is not a word boundary and reading a word at a time would need
//  every offset to be a special case.
uint byte_at(uint at) {
    return (w[at >> 2u] >> ((at & 3u) * 8u)) & 255u;
}

//  Four bytes of the weights as one word, from a run that need not begin on
//  a word boundary.
//
//  byte_at above is a word load, a shift and a mask for every byte, so a
//  format whose quants are read one byte at a time pays four loads where one
//  would do. Q8_0's own branch has read words since it was written; the
//  k-quants did not, and a device generating with a four-bit k-quant was
//  slower than one generating with the eight-bit format that moves twice the
//  bytes -- 1.870 s against 1.281 for sixty-four tokens, which is what said
//  the loads and not the bytes were the cost.
//
//  The shift is the run's offset within its first word and is the same for
//  every block of a row, so a caller works it out once and passes it in.
//  Zero is its own case because a shift of thirty-two is undefined.
uint word_of(uint at_word, uint shift) {
    uint first = w[at_word];

    return shift == 0u
         ? first
         : (first >> shift) | (w[at_word + 1u] << (32u - shift));
}

//  One byte read as a signed number, which is how Q8_0 keeps a quant and
//  Q6_K keeps a sub-block scale.
int signed_at(uint at) {
    int value = int(byte_at(at));
    return value > 127 ? value - 256 : value;
}

//  Four signed bytes out of one word, in the order they lie.
//
//  The same four values signed_at would give for four consecutive addresses,
//  and about a quarter of the work: one load and two shifts an element,
//  against a load, a shift, a mask, a compare and a subtract each. Shifting
//  a byte to the top of a signed integer and back down is what sign-extends
//  it, and it is exact where the unpacking built into the language is not --
//  unpackSnorm4x8 clamps, and would turn a quant of -128 into -127 without
//  saying so.
//
//  The decode is worth measuring rather than assuming: building this shader
//  with the eight-bit branch's decode replaced by a constant takes the
//  device prompt from 0.573 s to 0.427, so unpacking is about a quarter of
//  what this spends.
//  Kept as four expressions at the point of use rather than a vector
//  returned from here: a vector of four returned across a call is a vector
//  the compiler put in memory, and doing that measured sixteen per cent
//  worse than the byte-at-a-time decode it was meant to replace.

//  A half-precision number, which is how every packed format writes a scale
//  and how F16 writes a weight.
float half_at(uint at) {
    return unpackHalf2x16(byte_at(at) | (byte_at(at + 1u) << 8u)).x;
}

//  An E8M0 exponent byte as the power of two it names, less one. The bit
//  pattern of two to the byte less a hundred and twenty-eight has a biased
//  exponent of the byte less one and no mantissa, so the whole of it is a
//  shift; the two bytes below that are subnormal, where the shift is of
//  the leading bit instead. Exact, where a power taken at run time need
//  not be.
float e8m0_at(uint at) {
    uint e = byte_at(at);
    return uintBitsToFloat(e < 2u ? (0x00200000u << e) : ((e - 1u) << 23u));
}

